---
title: How to Create a Different Hiring Pipeline for Each Role
publishedAt: 2026-11-27
type: companies
description: >-
  Engineering and sales don't hire the same way. Build multiple hiring pipelines
  with your own stages, with examples and how to switch a job safely.
---
**Quick answer:** Use a separate hiring pipeline when two roles need different steps, such as a take-home for engineers and a role play for sales. Keep the stages few, give each one a clear exit rule, and reuse a pipeline across similar jobs. On GetHired a company can have up to 10 pipelines with up to 12 active stages each. Each job uses one, and you can switch or customise a job's pipeline without emailing candidates.

One shared set of stages works until your second kind of role. Then "Tech interview" appears on your sales job and nobody's sure what it means.

## What is a hiring pipeline?

A hiring pipeline is the ordered list of stages an applicant moves through, from applying to a final outcome. It shows where everyone stands and what happens next.

## Which hiring pipeline stages should each role have?

These are starting points to adapt:

{% table %}
-
- Engineering
- Sales
- Design
---
- Applied
- Applied
- Applied
---
- Resume screen
- Phone screen
- Portfolio review
---
- Take-home
- Role play
- Portfolio walkthrough
---
- Technical interview
- Manager interview
- Design exercise
---
- Final round
- References
- Team interview
---
- Offer
- Offer
- Offer
---
- Hired
- Hired
- Hired
{% /table %}

Every pipeline also needs a way out. GetHired's defaults are Applied, Screening, Interview, Offer and Hired, plus Rejected and On hold.

## How do you set up multiple pipelines on GetHired?

1. Open your company **Settings**, where pipelines are listed.
2. Create a pipeline. It starts as a copy of an existing one, your default unless you choose another, so it already has the essential stages.
3. Rename it, for example "Engineering", then add, rename, reorder or archive stages.
4. When you post a job, choose its pipeline in the **Pipeline** field. New jobs use your default if you don't pick.

One pipeline is always the default. Each keeps at least one Applied, Hired and Rejected stage. The list shows how many jobs use each pipeline.

**Editing a pipeline changes every job that uses it.** The editor warns you when more than one job shares it.

## What are stage types, and why do they matter?

You can name a stage anything. Its **type** decides which status the candidate sees, so your internal names stay internal.

{% table %}
-
- Stage type
- What the candidate's status shows
---
- Applied
- Submitted
---
- Screening, Interview, Offer
- Reviewed
---
- On hold
- On hold
---
- Hired
- Selected
---
- Rejected
- Rejected
{% /table %}

Renaming "Interview" to "Bar raiser" doesn't expose that name to applicants.

## How do you customise the pipeline for just one job?

Use **Customize for this job** in the job's Pipeline section. GetHired copies the job's current pipeline, names it after the job, moves the job to it and opens it for editing. Your other jobs aren't affected.

Do this for real one-off needs. If several jobs want the same change, create one shared pipeline instead, so you're not maintaining near-identical copies. The limit of 10 active pipelines includes these copies.

## Can you switch pipelines when a job already has applicants?

Yes. Each application moves to a stage in the new pipeline. By default each old stage maps to the first stage of the same type, and you can change the mapping in the preview.

There are safeguards:

- The new stage must give the candidate **the same status** they see now. A switch never changes what a candidate sees.
- **No emails** are sent by a switch.
- If the new pipeline lacks a needed stage, for example you have people On hold and it has no On hold stage, add one first.
- Open AI screening interviews aren't cancelled by the switch.
- The job's interview plan follows to the matching stages.

## Who can change hiring pipelines?

{% table %}
-
- Action
- Permission needed
---
- Create, edit, archive or customise pipelines
- Manage pipeline stages
---
- Choose or switch the pipeline on a job
- Edit that job
{% /table %}

Permissions come from each person's role. See [hiring as a team](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/hiring-as-a-team-custom-roles-permissions-and-hiring-teams-on-gethired).

## How do you keep pipelines useful?

- **Give every stage an exit rule.** "Move to Interview when the take-home meets the bar" beats "when it feels right".
- **Remove stages nobody uses.** An empty stage is noise on the board.
- **Name stages after what happens,** not after who does it.
- **Don't add a stage for every email.** Use notes and the timeline.
- **Check where people wait.** Median days in each stage on the job's funnel shows the bottleneck. See [recruiting metrics for startups](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/recruiting-metrics-for-startups-time-to-hire-funnel-conversion-and-source-of-hire).

Because jobs can have different stages, company-wide totals are grouped by stage type.

## Frequently asked questions

### How many stages should a hiring pipeline have?

Only as many as represent real decisions. Many small teams manage with five to seven. GetHired allows up to 12 active stages per pipeline.

### How many pipelines can a company have on GetHired?

Up to 10 active pipelines.

### Can I delete a pipeline?

You can archive a pipeline that isn't the default and isn't used by any job, including closed ones.

### Can the recruiter agent manage pipelines?

Yes. It can list and create pipelines, and set or customise a job's pipeline. Changing a job's pipeline waits for your approval, and the agent follows your role's permissions.

